# =======================================================================
# SURICATA TUNING: COMMON HOME / IOT / OFFLOADING FALSE POSITIVES
# =======================================================================
# suricata-update reads this file and strips matching SIDs before
# writing the final ruleset, so they never fire at runtime.

# --- 1. TCP/Stream Engine Anomalies (Caused by cheap IoT NICs & VM Offloading) ---
# When Linux GRO/LRO clumps packets together, or IoT devices hard-close connections,
# the Suricata stream-tracking engine gets pedantic and throws these protocol errors.
2260001  # SURICATA Applayer Wrong direction first Data
2210042  # SURICATA STREAM TIMEWAIT ACK with wrong seq
2210044  # SURICATA STREAM Packet with invalid timestamp
2210045  # SURICATA STREAM Packet with invalid ack
2210020  # SURICATA STREAM ESTABLISHED packet out of window
2210029  # SURICATA STREAM ESTABLISHED RST out of window
2210008  # SURICATA STREAM 3way handshake wrong seq wrong ack (Mobile devices roaming)
2210010  # SURICATA STREAM 3way handshake SYNACK with wrong ack
2210030  # SURICATA STREAM FIN invalid ack: common in noisy network environments; typically a transient state mismatch rather than malicious activity
2210038  # SURICATA STREAM FIN out of window: common in high-traffic environments and does not represent a security threat
2210046  # SURICATA STREAM SHUTDOWN RST invalid ack: common TCP stream engine alert in noisy network environments

# --- 2. Smart Home / IoT Broadcast Chatter ---
# Smart TVs, printers, and media players constantly yell their presence
# to the local network using UPnP and SSDP. These are safe on an internal VLAN.
2013028  # ET POLICY UPnP SUBSCRIBE method
2013031  # ET POLICY UPnP NOTIFY method
2224005  # SURICATA DNS malformed request data (mDNS / Bonjour traffic often triggers this)
2230009  # SURICATA HTTP response has no URI (Often triggered by weird SSDP responses)

# --- 3. Mobile Device / VoIP Traffic (STUN / WebRTC) ---
# WhatsApp, FaceTime, Discord, and WebRTC use STUN to punch holes through NAT.
# EmergingThreats flags these as "INFO", but mobile phones spam them constantly.
# The "non-standard high port" variant fires per-packet during ICE negotiation,
# generating 60+ alerts per failed P2P candidate before the client falls back to relay.
2011507  # ET INFO Session Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN Binding Request)
2016149  # ET INFO Session Traversal Utilities for NAT (STUN Binding Response)
2033078  # ET INFO STUN Binding Request On Non-Standard High Port: routine STUN traffic commonly associated with WebRTC and VoIP applications; expected behavior in modern network environments and does not indicate a security threat
2036220  # ET INFO Android Device Connectivity Check: standard Android connectivity checks are routine background traffic and do not indicate malicious activity or security incidents

# --- 4. Cloud / Protocol Diagnostic Noise ---
# Report: Monthly Suricata AI Triage (2026-03-03 through 2026-03-31, end exclusive), run_id=manual__2026-04-23T14:25:42.202385+00:00
2050736  # ET INFO Tencent Cloud Storage Domain in DNS Lookup: routine DNS lookups for Tencent Cloud storage domains are common infrastructure traffic and do not indicate malicious activity in this context
2052580  # ET INFO DNS Query to Alibaba Cloud CDN Domain: routine DNS queries to Alibaba Cloud CDN are common infrastructure traffic and do not indicate malicious activity in this context
2052581  # ET INFO Observed Alibaba Cloud CDN Domain in TLS SNI: routine traffic to Alibaba Cloud CDN is common infrastructure and does not indicate malicious activity in this context
2260002  # SURICATA Applayer Detect protocol only one direction: parser only observed traffic in one direction, which is common in incomplete sessions or specific protocol handshakes and does not represent a security threat
2260003  # SURICATA Applayer Protocol detection skipped: application layer protocol detection was skipped, which is a common informational message in Suricata when traffic does not match known protocol patterns or is malformed

# --- 5. TCP Session Noise from Broken Links / Lossy Paths ---
# These stream-engine alerts are common on asymmetric, lossy, or heavily NATed paths.
2210054  # SURICATA STREAM excessive retransmissions: network congestion or packet loss is common in standard network operations and does not represent a specific security threat requiring individual investigation

# --- 6. Harmless TLS / Crypto Warnings ---
# Modern browsers and apps frequently negotiate TLS closures abruptly, or use
# newer QUIC protocols that Suricata sometimes misclassifies.
2221010  # SURICATA TLS error message warning
2221029  # SURICATA TLS invalid record type
